,78],"logprob":-1.8596821973915212e-05,"token":"SN"},{"bytes":[115],"logprob":-0.0001435382873751223,"token":"s"},{"bytes":[32,105,110],"logprob":-0.64988112449646,"token":" in"},{"bytes":[32,118,97,114,105,111,117,115],"logprob":-0.44838955998420715,"token":" various"},{"bytes":[32,99,111,110,116,101,120,116,115],"logprob":-0.20377478003501892,"token":" contexts"},{"bytes":[46],"logprob":-0.36839884519577026,"token":"."}],"message":{"content":"Echo State Networks (ESNs) are a type of recurrent neural network used for processing time-series data. The network consists of a large, fixed set of nodes called a reservoir, which is typically sparsely connected and has random weights. Training involves learning the output weights only, making the training process faster. Reservoirs in ESNs are often trained using random connectivity and spectral radius tuning to ensure they have the right level of excitability.\n\nSummarizing the arXiv papers, none directly addressed echo state networks for anomaly detection, but papers like \"Integer Echo State Networks: Efficient Reservoir Computing for Digital Hardware\" and \"Evolutionary Echo State Network: evolving reservoirs in the Fourier space\" discuss the use and training of ESNs in various contexts.","role":"assistant"},"model":"qwen2.5:7b","prompt_eval_count":1242,"prompt_eval_duration":44732000,"total_duration":2141088500}}